00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:45.480
LINGYUN RUAN: Thanks, Ben.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.430
Hi, everyone.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.810
I'm Lingyun, and Snap engineer.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.850
So Snapchat is a best camera to
share the moment with friends.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.300
During the World Cup,
there were traffic spikes,

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.880
or backend, after
each important goal,

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.150
which means users were
sharing their excitement

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.840
at this exact moment by sending
Snaps or posting stories

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.350
with our top class lenses.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:13.300
Publishers also created a lot
of World Cup-related stories

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:14.620
for our user.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.470
We really want our users
to have the best product

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.174
experience on Snapchat.

00:03:19.174 --> 00:03:20.590
That's why we need
a strong server

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.815
infrastructure that assures high
availability and low latency.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.640
So to optimize the service for
different types of stories,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.380
we have this
complicated backend,

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.670
which consists of a great
number of components.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:38.360
Each component can depend on
multiple account services.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.030
To run a complicated
system like this well,

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:45.130
we need a good monitoring to
understand its performance.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:49.430
We also want to discover and
the root cause issues fast.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:51.090
But this is
especially challenging

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.430
if it will run your
service on the Cloud.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.910
Because often, you rely
on this Cloud products,

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:00.176
which are usually black
boxes in modern-term world.

00:04:00.176 --> 00:04:01.300
Let's simplify the problem.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.830
So if we take away all the
implementation details,

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.110
we'll see some simple
diagram like this.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.980
There are three
parts in the system--

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.390
client, our services,
and the Cloud products.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:18.149
Just like what people usually
do in the cloud world,

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:20.700
our services rely on
these Cloud products

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.420
to serve a request from client.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.110
And then we try to make the
best use of this Cloud product

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.170
so we can focus on
the implementation

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.480
of our own business logic.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.480
We wouldn't spend effort
re-inventing the wheels

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.870
as long as this Cloud product
can meet our service quality

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:43.240
requirement and are
also cost-efficient.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:47.080
To tell whether the whole
system is running well,

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:49.780
we would add monitoring
in different places.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.710
So first, we need to understand
that the overall healthiness

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.330
of this system.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.590
So we usually add
serving metrics

00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:00.055
including traffic, errors,
and latency to our service.

00:05:00.055 --> 00:05:04.500
This metrics at direct service
level indicators that tell us

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.820
how well the system is running.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:08.940
And then we also set up
alerts on these metrics

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:10.890
so we can get a
notification when

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.150
the serving quality degrades.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.540
During a outage, we want to
know which part of the system

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:19.420
is broken.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:22.310
And a service can have
multiple internal components.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:25.130
It can also depend
on other services.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:26.630
For these internal
components, we

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.270
can add custom metrics to
measure their performance

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.540
directly and tell
which path goes wrong.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:36.080
What about these
outgoing dependencies?

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.930
So people usually
add constant metrics

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:42.200
to measure the errors and
latency for the request that

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:45.190
to go to our dependent service.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.410
These constant
metrics are helpful.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.460
They are [INAUDIBLE] to indicate
whether we are having issue

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:52.370
calling our dependent server.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:57.010
But the problem is they don't
tell us where the issue lies.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:06:00.460
The issue can locate
either under caller side,

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.580
or the dependent server
side, or it can be

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.540
an actual issue in the middle.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:08.440
Thus, people usually wanted
to check their serving metrics

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.540
of this dependent service.

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.610
So it can compare them
with the [INAUDIBLE] ones.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:16.000
And only through this
kind of comparison,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.330
we can get a better picture
of what is going on.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.750
If we're on the
dependent service,

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:24.700
we can get into
some metrics easily,

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.580
by either adding
instrumentation, or just

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:29.805
issuing a monitoring agent
to correct these metrics

00:06:29.805 --> 00:06:31.750
if they're already available.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:35.560
But what if the dependent
service is a Cloud product?

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.660
In the traditional
cloud world, we

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:40.520
can't get a survey
metrics of these products.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:45.110
But now, Google is offering
visibility to its customers

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:47.050
under this transparent
SLI effort.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:53.060
So let's walk through
a for-real use case

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:56.200
to see how this transparent
SLI images are helping us

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.070
with production debugging.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:01.760
So let me use this simple
service as an example.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.450
So this is one of
the subcomponent

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:07.750
of the complicated story
service backend, which

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:09.800
is shown in previous slide.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.670
And this service is one
our story services that

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:16.070
serves stories metadata
in search result.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.220
So when you type something
in the search bar,

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.720
to reduce a
[? search ?] one size,

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.560
we don't include the full
metadata of these stories

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:25.090
immediately.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:26.730
The client will
only send a request

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.170
to fetch the full
metadata of that story.

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.420
Only when user clicks on story.

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:35.980
And we store these stories
full metadata in Cloud Spanner.

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:40.180
And we built this simple serving
layer and deployed on GKE.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:43.010
So this service just translated
request from client side

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:44.880
into Spanner
queries, and execute

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.650
to the queries on Spanner, and
then returns the result back

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:48.670
to the client.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.550
Very straightforward, right?

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:52.810
Then let's discuss
how we are going

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:57.520
to build a dashboard to monitor
this simple serving stack.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:00.710
So the most important question
we want to answer in this case

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:04.122
is, is this story
service performing well?

00:08:04.122 --> 00:08:08.310
Thus, we added our serving
metrics in our first row.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.960
From left to right, this
charts our request and errors,

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:16.620
error ratio, average latency,
and a 99% [INAUDIBLE] latency.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:19.620
These are all well known
as our [INAUDIBLE] signals.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.050
We can get a good sense
of the service level

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.680
with a just [INAUDIBLE]
single glance at this row.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.110
And this service just a
single [INAUDIBLE] of Spanner.

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:30.210
So when there is a
production issue,

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:32.700
the first thing we want to
check is whether these Spanner

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:34.380
requests are doing OK.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.789
So heuristically, we added
this Spanner constant metrics

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:40.270
into the second row.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:44.710
In the past, if we didn't see
something from these two rows,

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:47.440
we would start to guess.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:49.680
Maybe Spanner is having issue.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.210
But we could not be
100% sure about it.

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:57.910
Because this constant metrics
don't tell us the full picture.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:03.080
And in an outage our
users are impacted,

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:05.900
and we really want to
mitigate the problem

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:07.500
as soon as possible.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:09.560
And then we wish
we can see directly

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:11.090
behind the curtain
and to shorten

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:16.110
this procedure of verifying
different hypotheses.

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:16.610
Luckily--

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:19.085
[ALARM BELL]

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.450
Sorry.

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:24.550
Luckily, Google--

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:25.460
BEN TREYNOR: We don't
want to hear that.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:27.500
LINGYUN RUAN: Yeah, I don't
want to hear it right now.

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:28.930
BEN TREYNOR: I don't think
we have to hear that yet.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:30.620
Let's keep looking at this
issue on the dashboard

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:31.400
that you've got.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:33.120
LINGYUN RUAN: OK.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:37.130
So luckily, Google now is
offering us this visibility.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:40.760
So we can put this Spanner
transparent SLI metrics

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.200
on the third row.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:46.340
This metrics directs Google-side
performance indicators

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:49.190
of how well Spanner
is handling requests

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:51.470
from this particular
Snap service.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:53.810
We will see [INAUDIBLE]
how this metrics can

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:55.960
help us locate issue fast.

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.160
The rest rows are
resource metrics.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:09:59.710
We always want to
make sure resources

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.070
are provisioned properly.

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:03.290
In the first row,
we have the charts

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:08.460
which show Spanner resource
metrics, including,

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:12.020
per table Spanner
instance securitization

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:16.900
and per table operation rate
and errors In the last row,

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:19.590
we have the charts monitor
our services resource.

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.550
And from left to
right these charts

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:23.780
are-- per instance
CPU [INAUDIBLE],,

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:26.470
memory [INAUDIBLE],,
and network traffic.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:29.730
So in all, having a dashboard
with these five rows

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:31.280
we can answer the
question, is it

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:33.950
a Snap issue or a Google issue,
faster during our incident.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:40.570
Just the [? sign ?] over here,
Google exposes dashboard API,

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:43.150
so we can configure our
dashboard like this using code

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:46.320
and keep track of all the
changes in our code base.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:49.910
This feature is currently
under a earliest access program

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:50.910
and we really enjoy it.

00:10:54.806 --> 00:10:56.267
[ALARM BELL]

00:10:56.267 --> 00:10:57.764
BEN TREYNOR: Uh-oh.

00:10:57.764 --> 00:10:59.180
Lingyun, there's
that sound again.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:01.130
I think something might
actually be wrong.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.440
What are you going
to do about it?

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:06.330
LINGYUN RUAN: Let
me check the page.

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:07.070
Don't panic.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:08.450
Let's check the page.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.440
Oh, OK.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.980
This says our server ratio
has jumped significantly

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:14.510
for this story service.

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:15.395
Don't panic.

00:11:15.395 --> 00:11:19.270
Let's open the dashboard
and take a look.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:21.710
So now we are looking
at the dashboard

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:23.120
we just built together, right?

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.250
So remember in the old
days, we would have started

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:30.550
from these first set of rows.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:32.590
And let's see.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:34.550
In the first row,
are our story matrix.

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:37.120
We can see that our
ratio has jumped

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:40.680
with latency, which is a
common scenario in a production

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:41.650
outage.

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:44.970
And it's usually the case,
that either the processing

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:48.290
on the server side is slow
or there's slow backends.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:50.220
And number of errors
that is climb as we

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.100
request [INAUDIBLE] a timeout.

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:55.020
And then let's look at the
second row, or our Spanner

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:56.380
client metrics.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:58.970
And we can see, this
chart align real well

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:00.690
with this in the first row.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:07.010
Since Spanner requests are
taking longer to return,

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:08.760
is it a Google issue?

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:11.550
Usually, at this stage we
will file a support ticket

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:13.410
to check with the
support engineers.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:15.210
But now we don't have to.

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:19.610
Now we can look directly at
the third row, this Spanner

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:21.780
transparent SI metrics.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:22.690
And let's see.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:24.220
This says, no errors.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:26.952
And we can also see
some latency increase.

00:12:26.952 --> 00:12:30.540
But if we take a closer
look and compare the numbers

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:32.760
with the numbers
in the first rows,

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.670
we can find something
interesting.

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:37.320
Client latency
increased 10 times more

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:39.780
than what the
service side shows.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:43.890
So it implies maybe it's not
our issue on Spanner side.

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:45.770
And the Spanner side
latency increase

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:48.030
can be a result of a
slow client [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.720
So at this stage, let's continue
investigating on the Snap side

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:55.270
instead of filing a ticket.

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:57.990
So let's look further
down in the dashboard.

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:00.460
From this Spanner
resource metrics,

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:02.216
we can see our Spanner
instance actually

00:13:02.216 --> 00:13:04.090
are running with
reasonable CPU [INAUDIBLE],,

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:06.180
which is another signal
that it doesn't support

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:08.320
it's a Spanner-side issue.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:11.770
And then we move
on to this resource

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:13.570
metrics of our service.

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:15.350
Here we go.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:19.310
We found that when the incident
took place, the CPU [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:22.850
of one of the server instance
jumped to something crazy,

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:26.870
like 100% utilized,
while the CPU [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:30.340
for other instances dropped.

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:33.950
It usually indicates the
traffic is not balanced,

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.550
and the one single instance
is serving almost all

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:39.350
of the traffic,
which is pretty bad.

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:42.690
And we checked around
and found someone just

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:45.480
wrote the balance configuration
change, so let's just

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.078
fix it with a revert.

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:51.950
So we've just gone
through an example

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:54.320
of how we're using
different metrics to root

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:56.950
cause our products and issue.

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:58.850
This can be just
one possible version

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:01.130
of the story in which
this transparent SI

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:05.150
metrics helps us conclude
it's a Snap-side issue.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:08.620
And we save a lot of time by not
creating a false alarm ticket

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:09.740
to Google.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:12.500
As you may know, communicating
over the support ticket

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:15.710
is very time-consuming, even
if the support engineers

00:14:15.710 --> 00:14:17.690
are responsive.

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:19.530
This is because
support engineers

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:22.370
need to understand your
service, your environment,

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:25.310
and the real issue before
they can start investigation

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:26.450
on their side.

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:28.700
And it takes a lot of back
and forth communication

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:30.470
for this code [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:32.750
The whole story can
be a different one

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:35.960
if when we look at the
Spanner SI metrics and found

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:37.430
it's a Google-side issue.

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:39.860
In that case, we can
just create a ticket

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:43.610
and point the support
engineers to the exact metrics

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:47.030
we looked at, which also makes
the communication faster.

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:50.900
So as a summary, we really like
this transparent SI metrics,

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:53.420
as they give us
additional usability

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:56.720
and also help us save time
in production debugging,

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:59.251
no matter if it is a
Google issue or not.

00:14:59.251 --> 00:14:59.750
All right.

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:01.166
Thanks for listening
to our story.

00:15:01.166 --> 00:15:04.004
Let me hand it over back to Ben.

00:15:04.004 --> 00:15:05.916
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:07.967 --> 00:15:09.300
BEN TREYNOR: Thank you, Lingyun.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:12.270
So yes, as Lingyun
has just illustrated,

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:14.790
this sort of scenario
actually happens

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:16.410
every day in production, right?

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:18.330
Things work differently.

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:19.770
You have variations.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:21.510
And one of your first
jobs, especially

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:23.700
if you have that klaxon
going off in the background,

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.500
is to figure out what
has occurred, right?

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:27.694
Something has changed, but
was it a user behavior?

00:15:27.694 --> 00:15:28.860
Was it a push that you made?

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:31.140
Is something going
on in the platform?

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:33.300
Historically, the way
that this would get done

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.100
is you would look
at your application

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.209
and then file a ticket,
and 20 minutes later

00:15:37.209 --> 00:15:39.000
somebody from support
would get back to you

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.150
and you'd joint debug, and
all this time, time is passing

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:43.707
and your users
are being damaged.

00:15:43.707 --> 00:15:45.540
And if you can make
those intervals shorter,

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:48.060
if you can make the period
between when the klaxon goes

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:50.800
off and when your service is
working normal again, much,

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:53.220
much shorter, all of
your users are happier.

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:55.320
And your engineers
are more quickly

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.600
able to solve the problems
that they are introducing,

00:15:57.600 --> 00:15:59.070
either intentionally
or otherwise,

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:00.611
with the changes
that they're making.

00:16:00.611 --> 00:16:03.560
And so this introduction
of the Transparent SLI

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:06.156
and the Transparent API for
all of Google's services

00:16:06.156 --> 00:16:07.530
is a huge step in
that direction.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.902
It lets engineers at
companies like Snapchat

00:16:10.902 --> 00:16:12.860
spend more of their time
developing and rolling

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:15.600
out cool apps and less
time debugging problems.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.240
So I'm happy to announce today
that the Transparent SLIs are

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:20.850
available for all
Google services,

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:23.790
and they give you the same
key signals that we actually

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:25.530
have learned to use
internally at Google

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.630
in the site-reliability
team to manage all

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:29.770
of Google's infrastructures.

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:31.590
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:32.140
Thank you.

00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:32.640
Thank you.

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:38.380
You know, this both,
from my perspective,

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:42.280
helps everyone develop and keep
running their favorite services

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:45.970
on Google, and it helps us
apply a little bit of what we've

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:48.070
learned over the last 15
years of running Google's

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:51.250
internal cloud, and make
it easy for all of you

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:53.290
to develop your applications.

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:55.570
And it helps you minimize
the time between when

00:16:55.570 --> 00:16:58.035
something unexpected occurs,
and when you've accurately

00:16:58.035 --> 00:17:00.160
done a differential diagnosis
and have either said,

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:02.160
this is something that
we can change on our own,

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:03.860
or we need to contact Google.

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:05.349
And if you do
contact Google, you

00:17:05.349 --> 00:17:08.328
can send the screenshot that
your application monitoring has

00:17:08.328 --> 00:17:10.119
sped up, and we won't
spend any time trying

00:17:10.119 --> 00:17:11.140
to figure out what's going on.

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:12.973
We can dive right to
the heart of the issue.

00:17:12.973 --> 00:17:15.099
Finally, even in
developing applications

00:17:15.099 --> 00:17:16.540
when nothing is
going wrong, it's

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:19.240
very useful to be able
to see details of what

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:20.720
the backend services are doing.

00:17:20.720 --> 00:17:22.900
Not just the median
latency or the error rate,

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:24.520
but actually the
latency distribution,

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:26.290
and what happens over the
course of the last week.

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:28.630
And this lets you build
applications that are better

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:30.338
suited to the performance
characteristics

00:17:30.338 --> 00:17:32.830
of the infrastructure
that you're running on.

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:35.530
If you would like to
learn more about this

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:39.820
and other cool production
development practices,

