00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:37.550
What we can use, though,
is distributed tracing.

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:39.080
And distributed
tracing allows you

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:41.150
to track how a
request propagates

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:43.190
between different parts
of your application

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:45.050
as they actually happen.

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:48.680
So this scatterplot here
on the vertical axis

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:50.734
shows the latency of
captured requests.

00:20:50.734 --> 00:20:52.400
On the horizontal
axis, we have the time

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:53.630
in which they were captured.

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:56.131
And you can click between them
to view the different traces.

00:20:56.131 --> 00:20:57.755
And when you click
on one, you see what

00:20:57.755 --> 00:20:58.922
we call the waterfall graph.

00:20:58.922 --> 00:21:01.046
This waterfall graph shows
the different components

00:21:01.046 --> 00:21:01.620
of a request.

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:03.453
So in this case, it's
the different services

00:21:03.453 --> 00:21:08.010
that are hopped between
as the trace was captured.

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:09.810
And so from here,
we can see that this

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:11.730
was a call made to
the cart endpoint

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:13.212
of the front-end
service, and then

00:21:13.212 --> 00:21:15.420
it went to the currency
service, and recommendations,

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:18.060
and finally it started going
to product catalog service.

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:19.780
Now we see two things
are a problem here,

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:21.990
and we went over this in
this morning's keynote.

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.476
The first is that we're making
a ton of separate requests

00:21:24.476 --> 00:21:25.600
to product catalog service.

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:27.650
This just seems like
bad design, right like?

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:31.350
Like if we're viewing a set of
data for a particular product

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:33.202
or looking at
something for the cart,

00:21:33.202 --> 00:21:34.910
we should probably be
calling the catalog

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:37.170
a single batch request if
there's multiple products.

00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:39.420
And if there's only one
product, we should really only

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:40.260
be calling it once.

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:41.560
That's going to
take a while to fix.

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:42.990
Like, there's some real
re-engineering there,

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:45.073
and I'm not going to bore
you to tears in the demo

00:21:45.073 --> 00:21:46.410
while I rewrite the application.

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:48.201
But the other thing
that we can investigate

00:21:48.201 --> 00:21:50.560
is why these individual
requests are slow.

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:52.650
So even if we batched
these up, this

00:21:52.650 --> 00:21:54.090
would take still far too long.

00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:56.700
Our trace that had--

00:21:56.700 --> 00:21:58.230
what's that, 10
seconds of latency--

00:21:58.230 --> 00:22:00.959
would still have well over one
second if we batch together

00:22:00.959 --> 00:22:02.250
these product catalog requests.

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:04.250
You can see this individual
component is already

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:06.060
1 and 1/2 seconds long.

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:09.750
And so the trace doesn't help
us go any deeper than that.

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:12.990
You can add custom components
to the trace using our APIs,

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:15.120
but still, generally
you won't understand how

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:16.530
the code is actually running.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:19.810
And so to do that, we can
jump to Stackdriver Profiler.

00:22:19.810 --> 00:22:22.580
Stackdriver Profiler is a
continuous production profiler.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:25.830
It's the newest component
of our APM suite.

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:27.480
To you, as a developer,
you basically

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:29.479
turn it on your application
and forget about it.

00:22:29.479 --> 00:22:31.380
And it continually
captures information

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:34.830
about how your code actually
executes in production.

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:36.600
The nice thing about
Stackdriver Profiler

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:38.940
is that compared to similar
entries in the market,

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:41.260
it has effectively no
performance impact,

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:43.774
so you could just leave it
running in the background,

00:22:43.774 --> 00:22:44.940
which it will sample itself.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:46.689
It'll turn on and off
and various services

00:22:46.689 --> 00:22:47.754
at different times.

00:22:47.754 --> 00:22:49.170
And to you as a
developer, there's

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:51.290
basically no performance cost,
which is why it's really nice.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:52.650
And you don't have
to worry about it.

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:53.858
You don't have to turn it on.

00:22:53.858 --> 00:22:56.105
You don't have to start
profiling sessions at random.

00:22:56.105 --> 00:22:59.370
It supports Heap, thread
profiling, and CPU profiling,

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:01.110
depending on the
language that you're on.

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:02.995
It will also do
wall time profiling.

00:23:02.995 --> 00:23:04.620
We're going to jump
into CPU profiling.

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:06.880
And we can see
something right away.

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:10.320
The Get Product endpoint of this
service, the gRPC method for it

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:11.760
is really, really slow.

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:14.370
It's consuming 92%
of our system's CPU.

00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:16.710
If we run a language that
allowed all-time profiling,

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:18.124
we'd also see that.

00:23:18.124 --> 00:23:20.790
And we can see that effectively,
all the time for it is taken up

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:23.990
by this parse catalog method.

00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:26.110
And so what we can do
now is dive into the code

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:28.590
and see what's actually
happening there.

00:23:28.590 --> 00:23:33.469
So this is the parse
catalog code here.

00:23:33.469 --> 00:23:35.260
This is inside our
product catalog service.

00:23:35.260 --> 00:23:36.790
It's written in Go.

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:39.330
And we can see here when it's
called it basically just does

00:23:39.330 --> 00:23:41.370
some JSON de-serialization.

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:43.650
And so from going from
trace, we understood

00:23:43.650 --> 00:23:46.060
which service was calling a
slowdown for our application.

00:23:46.060 --> 00:23:47.190
Without distributed
tracing that would

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:48.660
have been incredibly difficult.

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:51.420
And now with Profiler, we found
the actual function in our code

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:52.860
that's slowing everything down.

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:55.026
So in just a few minutes,
or a few seconds depending

00:23:55.026 --> 00:23:56.740
on how quickly you
went through this,

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:59.301
we can find out exactly
where our slowdown is caused.

00:23:59.301 --> 00:24:01.550
And this might seem like a
bit of a contrived example.

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:02.940
Like, in fact, if you
looked at the code,

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:05.180
you'd see that I made this
use JSON serialization

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:07.010
to slow it down for a demo.

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:08.850
But for when we talk
to our customers,

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:10.399
this isn't so outlandish.

00:24:10.399 --> 00:24:12.690
We've talked to many customers
where their services are

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:14.250
slow because
they're just burning

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:17.940
CPU time on serialization,
de-serialization,

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:20.550
or where they have things like
regular expressions, or just

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:22.530
functions that, you
know, you wouldn't even

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:25.190
expect to show up as
a blip on a CPU chart

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:29.040
or really a list of things
that are slowing things down.

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:32.830
And it's consuming 10%, 20%,
30% of their system's CPU.

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:34.527
And so this is
actually an example

00:24:34.527 --> 00:24:36.360
that's really, really
appropriate for things

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:38.870
that our customers
have run into.

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:41.580
And so in the demo this morning
that I did at the keynote,

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:44.370
we then redeployed the
service and fix the issue.

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:46.952
So I'm not going to do that
here and repeat everything.

00:24:46.952 --> 00:24:49.410
What I will show you is that
between the different versions

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:51.480
of this code, we can
compare the performance

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:54.960
with the new Stackdriver
Profiler comparison feature.

00:24:54.960 --> 00:24:56.790
And Stackdriver
Profiler comparisons

00:24:56.790 --> 00:24:59.190
allows you to compare
profiles from your services

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:01.880
either across versions, or
across different time periods,

00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:03.480
or across different regions.

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:05.610
And from this, we can
see that the slow code

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:08.160
here, particularly
our parse catalog,

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:10.482
has gone to,
effectively, 0% CPU.

00:25:10.482 --> 00:25:12.690
And even the get product
endpoint, which of course is

00:25:12.690 --> 00:25:15.720
still there in the new version,
is now down to effectively 0%

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:16.459
CPU.

00:25:16.459 --> 00:25:18.750
And we can use this to verify
that our code changes had

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:20.272
the impact that we expected.

00:25:20.272 --> 00:25:22.230
Obviously, if you were
doing this in real life,

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:25.170
you'd want to start by doing the
metrics comparison of latency

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:27.050
just to verify that your
changes have worked.

00:25:27.050 --> 00:25:29.341
But with this, we know that
the changes inside the code

00:25:29.341 --> 00:25:33.615
have actually had the
effect that we wanted.

00:25:33.615 --> 00:25:35.890
For Stackdriver Trace, we
also have a similar feature

00:25:35.890 --> 00:25:37.639
where you can compare
latency across time,

00:25:37.639 --> 00:25:40.680
or across versions, or
across different areas.

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:42.380
So I mentioned that
Stackdriver APM

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:44.210
is composed of three products.

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:46.690
So I mentioned
Stackdriver Trace,

00:25:46.690 --> 00:25:48.560
I walked you through
Stackdriver Profiler.

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:50.540
Finally, we have
Stackdriver Debugger.

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:52.880
Stackdriver Debugger,
just like the other tools,

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:55.790
you can run safely and
continuously in production.

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:59.570
It is very sort of
magical in a sense.

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:02.480
It lets you set breakpoints
in your production environment

00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:03.020
safely.

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:05.134
We call these snapshots.

00:26:05.134 --> 00:26:06.800
And so the best way
to show this is just

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:08.147
to give a demonstration.

00:26:08.147 --> 00:26:10.730
So the code I'm looking at here
is for the currency conversion

00:26:10.730 --> 00:26:11.360
service.

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:13.359
This is a service where
you can call in, give it

00:26:13.359 --> 00:26:15.069
a value in US
dollars and tell it

00:26:15.069 --> 00:26:16.860
I want to know what
this is worth in euros.

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:19.190
The Hipster Shop store
lets you change currencies.

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:21.670
The currency service
is what performs this.

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:24.170
So one of the challenges that
you might have with a currency

00:26:24.170 --> 00:26:26.717
conversion service is that it
might be converting currencies

00:26:26.717 --> 00:26:28.550
in a different manner
than you expect, or it

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:30.890
might be converting them
with the wrong values.

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:33.740
So the way the service works
is every hour or so it goes

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:36.392
to a web service, I think hosted
by the European Central Bank

00:26:36.392 --> 00:26:38.600
or something like that, that
lists their up-to-o-date

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:40.150
currency conversion rates.

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:42.525
So let's imagine that
you looked at those rates

00:26:42.525 --> 00:26:45.150
and you looked at the values of
the currency conversion service

00:26:45.150 --> 00:26:47.180
was outputting and
they didn't match.

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:49.397
Today, to debug
this, you'd probably

00:26:49.397 --> 00:26:51.230
add a bunch of log
statements to the service

00:26:51.230 --> 00:26:53.750
to print out what it thought
the current currency conversion

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:54.860
values were.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:56.360
You'd go and redeploy
the app, which

00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:58.443
might take a while, depending
on your environment,

00:26:58.443 --> 00:27:00.425
and then you'd see the
output of those logs.

00:27:00.425 --> 00:27:03.200
With Stackdriver Debugger,
we can short-circuit that.

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:07.034
All I have to do is click here
on line 123 in the interface.

00:27:07.034 --> 00:27:08.450
We've already
captured a snapshot.

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:10.237
There's a load test
running against this.

00:27:10.237 --> 00:27:12.320
And from here, we can look
at the local variables,

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:14.960
like this data objects,
and see the actual values

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:17.559
that the service thinks, in
memory, that it should be

00:27:17.559 --> 00:27:18.850
converting these currencies at.

00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:21.920
So right away you can debug
it and verify your hypothesis

00:27:21.920 --> 00:27:24.650
that the currency conversion
values were wrong.

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:26.410
You can verify if
that was correct.

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:28.160
We also, just like a
traditional debugger,

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:29.600
support full
conditions so you can

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:32.540
tell it to only set a snapshot
if certain variables are

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:34.100
set to certain values.

00:27:34.100 --> 00:27:36.680
And you can use expressions
to inspect the output.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.930
Finally, you can navigate
through the different frames

00:27:38.930 --> 00:27:39.780
in the call stack.

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:40.940
I admit that this is
probably not the greatest

00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:43.562
example because this node
function calls itself a lot,

00:27:43.562 --> 00:27:45.020
but you can also
view the variables

00:27:45.020 --> 00:27:45.960
within those different frames.

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:47.376
So it gives you
the power that you

00:27:47.376 --> 00:27:49.884
expect of a regular debugger
without halting execution,

00:27:49.884 --> 00:27:52.050
without interrupting your
customer's workflow, which

00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:54.230
is really, really,
really excellent.

00:27:54.230 --> 00:27:57.055
We use technologies like this,
Profiler And trace every day

00:27:57.055 --> 00:27:59.930
at Google, and we're really
excited to show these

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:01.890
to our customers.

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:03.895
So we should talk about
two things with these.

00:28:03.895 --> 00:28:05.520
The first question
that always comes up

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:07.950
is, how easy is this to set up?

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:10.962
I can tell you from experience,
from working on this demo app,

00:28:10.962 --> 00:28:12.420
as well as just,
of course, working

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:14.794
on these products a lot, it's
very, very straightforward.

00:28:14.794 --> 00:28:17.460
So we do in-process
instrumentation, simply

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:20.042
libraries that you link to your
application and redeploy it.

00:28:20.042 --> 00:28:21.750
All of our instrumentation
is open source

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:25.440
and Apache licensed on
GitHub across all languages.

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:30.480
For Trace and for Debugger,
we support Java, Go, Node.js,

00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:33.270
.NET, Ruby, Python, and PHP.

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:34.770
So we have a set
of seven languages.

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:37.610
For Trace, we
additionally support C++.

00:28:37.610 --> 00:28:39.119
For Profiler, it's
relatively new.

00:28:39.119 --> 00:28:41.160
So we're still building
out the language support.

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:42.747
So we support
Java, Go, and Node,

00:28:42.747 --> 00:28:44.830
and we're developing support
for Python right now,

00:28:44.830 --> 00:28:48.700
and then we'll continue
looking at the others.

00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:50.464
For Stackdriver Trace,
our instrumentation

00:28:50.464 --> 00:28:52.380
is through a project
that we started at Google

00:28:52.380 --> 00:28:54.540
and we still run
called OpenCensus.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:57.660
OpenCensus is a set of
language-specific libraries

00:28:57.660 --> 00:29:00.240
that will automatically
capture traces

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:02.430
and application-level
metrics from applications.

00:29:02.430 --> 00:29:04.770
So for example, in
this application

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:07.019
here, by adding
OpenCensus to one

00:29:07.019 --> 00:29:09.060
of the Go parts of the
application of these lines

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:11.070
of code here, we
automatically captured

00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:14.040
traces, propagated contexts,
embedded annotations

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:16.290
and attributes and those
traces, and we automatically

00:29:16.290 --> 00:29:17.748
captured
application-level metrics.

00:29:17.748 --> 00:29:19.530
So it's really easy
to get started with.

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:22.890
We have integrations across
all these languages for things

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:26.130
like GRPC, various HTTP
clients, SQL clients,

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:28.690
other storage clients,
client libraries for Google,

00:29:28.690 --> 00:29:29.510
and so on.

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:32.160
And we try to make it as
automatic as possible.

00:29:32.160 --> 00:29:33.660
OpenCensus is also
great because we

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:34.740
have this concept of exporters.

00:29:34.740 --> 00:29:36.115
And this is the
reason OpenCensus

00:29:36.115 --> 00:29:38.670
is a community project rather
than just a Google project.

00:29:38.670 --> 00:29:40.170
So when you set
up OpenCensus, we

00:29:40.170 --> 00:29:41.910
told it to export
to Stackdriver,

00:29:41.910 --> 00:29:44.452
but if you were using
other APM solutions--

00:29:44.452 --> 00:29:45.660
we mentioned Datadog earlier.

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:48.270
You can export to
Datadog by OpenCensus.

00:29:48.270 --> 00:29:52.020
Or Prometheus, or Zipkin,
or Solarwinds, or others.

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:54.580
You can export your data
to your backend of choice.

00:29:54.580 --> 00:29:56.663
And with OpenCensus, you
can export your data even

00:29:56.663 --> 00:29:59.500
to multiple backends at the same
time, which is really powerful.

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:01.410
So we've made this really
easy to get started with.

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:03.180
You simply link a few
libraries, including

00:30:03.180 --> 00:30:04.772
OpenCensus to your application.

00:30:04.772 --> 00:30:07.230
They'll automatically capture
the right telemetry, redeploy

00:30:07.230 --> 00:30:07.830
your app.

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:09.690
You can get started
with Stackdriver APM.

00:30:09.690 --> 00:30:12.900
The pricing on Stackdriver
APM is extremely competitive.

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:15.260
Debugger and Profiler
are currently free.

00:30:15.260 --> 00:30:16.844
Trace has a pretty
generous free tier,

00:30:16.844 --> 00:30:18.676
and then there's a paid
tier after that that

00:30:18.676 --> 00:30:20.040
goes into effect in September.

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:22.050
Compared to competing
solutions, it's

00:30:22.050 --> 00:30:25.550
like a tiny minuscule
fraction of their cost.

00:30:25.550 --> 00:30:27.717
So with that, I'm done the demo.

00:30:27.717 --> 00:30:29.300
And we'll move on
to the next speaker.

00:30:29.300 --> 00:30:30.190
Thank you very much.

00:30:30.190 --> 00:30:31.390
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:34.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

